          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Teaching and Advocacy</strong><br>\nThroughout his career, Adams dedicated time to teaching photography and advocating for the medium as a form of art. He conducted workshops, lectured extensively, and wrote instructional guides on photographic techniques and philosophy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Style and Philosophy</strong><br>\nAdams's photographic style is marked by a careful composition, a wide range of tonal values, and a preference for large-format cameras. His philosophy emphasized the camera as a tool to capture an artist's vision rather than just to record a scene.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nAnsel Adams passed away in 1984, but his work continues to be celebrated for its technical precision, artistic beauty, and depth of emotion. His photographs and advocacy played a crucial role in the evolution of the modern environmental movement and remain influential in art and conservation circles.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
